{"answers": [{"question_id": "Q1", "question": "What event or condition triggers the scheduling of a vendor meeting — is it recurring on a fixed schedule, initiated by a specific operational issue, or requested on an ad hoc basis?", "answer": "The meeting is triggered by an improvement request or an equipment issue summary, suggesting it is initiated by a specific operational need rather than a fixed recurring schedule or purely ad hoc basis. The equipment engineer prepares an agenda and issue summary first, which then prompts the meeting organizer to schedule the vendor meeting."}, {"question_id": "Q2", "question": "Which roles from your organization typically attend the vendor meeting, and who holds primary responsibility for organizing it and producing the post-meeting summary?", "answer": "The equipment engineer and the vendor representative participate in discussing improvement options. The meeting organizer holds primary responsibility for scheduling the meeting and producing the post-meeting summary. The manager is also involved after the meeting to assign action-item owners and due dates."}, {"question_id": "Q3", "question": "What does the meeting summary look like as a finished output — is it a free-text email, a structured template, or a formal document — and who receives it?", "answer": "The output is meeting minutes shared via a shared document repository. The specific format (free-text, template, or formal document) is not specified in detail. The meeting organizer shares the summary after the manager assigns action-item owners and due dates. Recipients beyond the immediate team are not explicitly specified."}, {"question_id": "Q4", "question": "When action items are identified from the meeting, how are they recorded and assigned — do they include a designated owner, a due date, and a priority level, and if so, what tool or system is used to store this information?", "answer": "Action items are recorded with a designated owner and a due date assigned by the manager. Priority level is not specified. The action-item tracker is the system used to store this information, and items are also captured in the shared document repository alongside the meeting minutes."}, {"question_id": "Q5", "question": "How is follow-up on action items currently conducted, and what happens when an item is not completed by its agreed deadline — is there a formal escalation path, an automated reminder, or does someone follow up manually?", "answer": "The action-item owner is responsible for tracking progress. Automation of reminders and status tracking is identified as an opportunity, implying it is not fully automated today. A missed follow-up deadline is listed as a known exception. A formal escalation path is not explicitly specified in the workflow."}, {"question_id": "Q6", "question": "What criteria must be met for this workflow to be considered fully complete — for example, all action items marked closed, a final summary report approved, or some other condition?", "answer": "The workflow concludes when action-item progress is tracked by the action-item owner following the shared meeting summary. Explicit completion criteria such as all items marked closed or a final report approved are not specified. The workflow does require all action items to have clear owners; missing owners are treated as an exception requiring resolution."}]}
